PA Society. Before joining TheTaxCalc, Rachel spent 8 years at a Big Four accounting firm advising high-net-worth clients on tax-efficient wealth strategies.\",\"knowsAbout\":[\"Federal Income Tax\",\"State Income Tax\",\"FICA Tax\",\"Payroll Tax\",\"IRS Compliance\",\"Tax Planning\",\"Multi-State Taxation\",\"Tax-Advantaged Accounts\"],\"sameAs\":[\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/rachelmitchellcpa/\",\"https://www.aicpa-cima.com/\"],\"worksFor\":{\"@type\":\"Organization\",\"name\":\"TheTaxCalc\",\"url\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com\"},\"image\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com/author-rachel-mitchell.webp\"},{\"@id\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com/blog/what-is-taxable-income-guide-2026#article\",\"@type\":\"Article\",\"headline\":\"What Is Taxable Income? Complete Guide (2026)\",\"description\":\"What do you get taxed on? Complete guide to taxable and tax-exempt income in 2026. 20 types of taxable income, 20 types of tax-free income, and 12 types of taxes.\",\"image\":{\"@type\":\"ImageObject\",\"url\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com/opengraph-image.png\",\"width\":1200,\"height\":630},\"datePublished\":\"2026-06-21T15:00:00.000Z\",\"dateModified\":\"2026-06-21T15:00:00.000Z\",\"author\":{\"@id\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com/blog/what-is-taxable-income-guide-2026#author\"},\"publisher\":{\"@id\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com/#organization\"},\"mainEntityOfPage\":{\"@type\":\"WebPage\",\"@id\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com/blog/what-is-taxable-income-guide-2026\"},\"keywords\":[\"what\",\"is\",\"taxable\",\"income\",\"guide\",\"2026\",\"taxable income\",\"tax exempt\",\"what do you get taxed on\",\"types of taxes\",\"hourly wage after tax\"],\"wordCount\":1291,\"articleSection\":\"Tax Guide\"},{\"@id\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com/blog/what-is-taxable-income-guide-2026#breadcrumb\",\"@type\":\"BreadcrumbList\",\"itemListElement\":[{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":1,\"name\":\"Home\",\"item\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":2,\"name\":\"Blog\",\"item\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com/blog\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":3,\"name\":\"What Is Taxable Income? Complete Guide (2026)\",\"item\":\"https://thetaxcalc.com/blog/what-is-taxable-income-guide-2026\"}]}]}</script><nav aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\" class=\"mb-6 flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm text-muted-foreground\"><a class=\"hover:text-foreground transition-colors\" href=\"/\">Home</a><span class=\"text-muted-foreground\" aria-hidden=\"true\">/</span><a class=\"hover:text-foreground transition-colors\" href=\"/blog\">Blog</a><span class=\"text-muted-foreground\" aria-hidden=\"true\">/</span><span class=\"truncate text-foreground font-medium max-w-[200px] sm:max-w-none\">What Is Taxable Income? Complete Guide (2026)</span></nav><article class=\"mb-8\"><div class=\"mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-4 mb-8\"><div class=\"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2\"><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800 dark:bg-emerald-500/20 dark:text-emerald-400 border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-500/30\">Tax Guide</span></div><h1 class=\"text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-foreground sm:text-4xl leading-tight\">What Is Taxable Income? Complete Guide (2026)</h1><p class=\"mt-2 text-base text-muted-foreground\">A CPA-reviewed guide by Rachel Mitchell, CPA — updated for 2026 tax year</p><p class=\"text-lg text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed\">What do you get taxed on? Complete guide to taxable and tax-exempt income in 2026. 20 types of taxable income, 20 types of tax-free income, and 12 types of taxes.</p><div class=\"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 text-sm text-muted-foreground border-b border-border/30 pb-4\"><span>By <!-- -->Rachel Mitchell<!-- -->, <!-- -->CPA</span><span><time dateTime=\"2026-06-21T15:00:00.000Z\">June 21, 2026</time></span><span>7<!-- --> min read</span></div><div class=\"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2\"><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/50 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\">what</span><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/50 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\">is</span><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/50 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\">taxable</span><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/50 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\">income</span><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/50 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\">guide</span><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/50 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\">2026</span><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/50 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\">taxable income</span><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/50 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\">tax exempt</span><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/50 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\">what do you get taxed on</span><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/50 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\">types of taxes</span><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/50 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\">hourly wage after tax</span></div></div><div class=\"mx-auto max-w-5xl lg:flex lg:gap-8\"><aside class=\"hidden lg:block lg:w-64 lg:shrink-0\"><div class=\"sticky top-24\"><nav aria-label=\"Table of contents\" class=\"space-y-1\"><p class=\"text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground mb-3\">On this page</p><ul class=\"space-y-1 text-sm\"><li><a href=\"#official-source\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Official Source</a></li><li><a href=\"#what-items-do-you-pay-taxes-on\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">What Items Do You Pay Taxes On?</a></li><li><a href=\"#fully-taxable-income\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Fully Taxable Income</a></li><li><a href=\"#partially-taxable-income\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Partially Taxable Income</a></li><li><a href=\"#what-income-is-exempt-from-taxes\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">What Income Is Exempt From Taxes?</a></li><li><a href=\"#tax-free-income-federal\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors pl-4 text-xs border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Tax-Free Income (Federal)</a></li><li><a href=\"#what-are-the-12-types-of-taxes-in-the-us\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">What Are the 12 Types of Taxes in the US?</a></li><li><a href=\"#what-is-the-minimum-salary-to-pay-income-tax\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">What Is the Minimum Salary to Pay Income Tax?</a></li><li><a href=\"#what-income-pays-37-in-taxes\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">What Income Pays 37% in Taxes?</a></li><li><a href=\"#what-is-the-60-trap\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">What Is the 60% Trap?</a></li><li><a href=\"#what-are-10-types-of-taxable-income\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">What Are 10 Types of Taxable Income?</a></li><li><a href=\"#can-i-gift-money-to-my-wife\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Can I Gift Money to My Wife?</a></li><li><a href=\"#how-do-i-know-if-i-have-to-pay-taxes\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">How Do I Know If I Have to Pay Taxes?</a></li><li><a href=\"#related-resources\" class=\"block py-1 text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors text-sm border-l-2 border-transparent pl-2\">Related Resources</a></li></ul></nav></div></aside><div class=\"mx-auto max-w-3xl lg:mx-0 lg:flex-1\"><div class=\"prose-container\"><p>\"What do you get taxed on?\" is one of the most common tax questions in America. The answer is more complex than you might think — some income is fully taxable, some is partially taxable, and some is completely tax-free. This 2026 guide explains every type of taxable and tax-exempt income, with clear examples.</p><h2 id=\"official-source\">Official Source</h2><p>Taxable income rules are defined by the IRS in <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Publication 525</a> and <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p17\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Publication 17</a>. Tax-exempt income is listed in <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525#en_US_2024_publink1000229723\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">IRS Publication 525, Chapter 2</a>.</p><h2 id=\"what-items-do-you-pay-taxes-on\">What Items Do You Pay Taxes On?</h2><h3 id=\"fully-taxable-income\">Fully Taxable Income</h3><p>These income types are 100% subject to federal income tax:</p><ol><li><strong>Wages and salaries</strong> — All employment income (W-2)</li><li><strong>Self-employment income</strong> — 1099 income, freelance, contractor (minus business expenses)</li><li><strong>Bonuses and commissions</strong> — Taxed as ordinary income</li><li><strong>Tips</strong> — All tips over $20/month per employer</li><li><strong>Vacation pay</strong> — Taxed as wages</li><li><strong>Severance pay</strong> — Taxed as wages</li><li><strong>Unemployment compensation</strong> — Fully taxable at federal level (some states exempt)</li><li><strong>Capital gains</strong> — Profit from selling investments (short-term at ordinary rates, long-term at 0/15/20%)</li><li><strong>Dividends</strong> — Qualified (15/20%) and ordinary (ordinary income rate)</li><li><strong>Interest income</strong> — Bank interest, bond interest (some municipal bonds exempt)</li><li><strong>Rental income</strong> — Rent received (minus expenses)</li><li><strong>Pension income</strong> — Most pensions are fully taxable</li><li><strong>401(k)/Traditional IRA withdrawals</strong> — Fully taxable as ordinary income</li><li><strong>Annuities</strong> — Portion of annuity payments (depends on basis)</li><li><strong>Gambling winnings</strong> — Fully taxable (lottery, casino, sports betting)</li><li><strong>Alimony received</strong> — Taxable for divorces finalized after 2018</li><li><strong>Jury duty pay</strong> — Taxable as miscellaneous income</li><li><strong>Prizes and awards</strong> — Fair market value is taxable</li><li><strong>Cancellation of debt</strong> — Generally taxable (exceptions exist)</li><li><strong>Royalties</strong> — Taxable as ordinary income</li></ol><h3 id=\"partially-taxable-income\">Partially Taxable Income</h3><ol><li><strong>Social Security benefits</strong> — 0%, 50%, or 85% taxable depending on combined income</li><li><strong>Annuities</strong> — Only the earnings portion is taxable (return of principal is tax-free)</li><li><strong>Non-qualified dividends</strong> — Taxed at ordinary rate (qualified at lower rate)</li><li><strong>Scholarships</strong> — Tuition/fees tax-free; room/board taxable</li></ol><h2 id=\"what-income-is-exempt-from-taxes\">What Income Is Exempt From Taxes?</h2><h3 id=\"tax-free-income-federal\">Tax-Free Income (Federal)</h3><p>These income types are <strong>not subject to federal income tax</strong>:</p><ol><li><strong>Roth IRA withdrawals</strong> — Qualified withdrawals (59.5+ and 5-year rule) are 100% tax-free</li><li><strong>Roth 401(k) withdrawals</strong> — Same as Roth IRA</li><li><strong>Municipal bond interest</strong> — Interest from state/local government bonds is federal tax-free</li><li><strong>Social Security</strong> (low income) — If combined income < $25,000 (single) or $32,000 (married)</li><li><strong>Veterans' benefits</strong> — Disability compensation, pensions, and education benefits</li><li><strong>Workers' compensation</strong> — Job-related injury/illness benefits</li><li><strong>Child support payments</strong> — Not taxable to the recipient</li><li><strong>Gifts received</strong> — Gifts under $18,000/year per giver (2026)</li><li><strong>Inheritances</strong> — No federal income tax on inherited money</li><li><strong>Life insurance proceeds</strong> — Not taxable as income to beneficiary</li><li><strong>Scholarships</strong> — Tuition, fees, books, supplies (not room/board)</li><li><strong>Welfare benefits</strong> — Public assistance payments</li><li><strong>Supplemental Security Income (SSI)</strong> — Not taxable</li><li><strong>Foster care payments</strong> — For qualified foster individuals</li><li><strong>Disability insurance</strong> — If premiums paid with after-tax dollars</li><li><strong>IRS tax refunds</strong> — Federal tax refunds are not taxable income</li><li><strong>Combat pay</strong> — Military pay earned in a combat zone</li><li><strong>Foreign income exclusion</strong> — Up to $130,000 (2026) if living abroad</li><li><strong>Home sale exclusion</strong> — Up to $250,000 (single) / $500,000 (married) profit tax-free</li><li><strong>HSA withdrawals</strong> — For qualified medical expenses</li></ol><h2 id=\"what-are-the-12-types-of-taxes-in-the-us\">What Are the 12 Types of Taxes in the US?</h2><div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Tax Type</th><th>Who Pays</th><th>Rate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Federal income tax</td><td>All earners</td><td>10-37%</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>State income tax</td><td>Residents of 41 states</td><td>0-13.3%</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>FICA (Social Security)</td><td>All employees</td><td>6.2% (employer matches)</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Medicare tax</td><td>All employees</td><td>1.45% (employer matches)</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>State sales tax</td><td>Consumers in 45 states</td><td>0-9.56%</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Property tax</td><td>Property owners</td><td>0.28-2.49%</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Capital gains tax</td><td>Investors</td><td>0/15/20%</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Estate tax</td><td>Estates over $13.99M</td><td>40%</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Gift tax</td><td>Gifts over $18,000/year</td><td>18-40%</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Excise tax</td><td>Consumers (gas, alcohol, tobacco)</td><td>Varies</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Self-employment tax</td><td>Self-employed</td><td>15.3%</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Corporate income tax</td><td>C-Corporations</td><td>21% (federal)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 id=\"what-is-the-minimum-salary-to-pay-income-tax\">What Is the Minimum Salary to Pay Income Tax?</h2><p>You must file a tax return (and potentially pay tax) if your income exceeds:</p><div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table><thead><tr><th>Filing Status</th><th>2026 Filing Threshold</th><th>Standard Deduction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Single (under 65)</td><td>$16,100</td><td>$16,100</td></tr><tr><td>Single (65+)</td><td>$17,700</td><td>$17,700</td></tr><tr><td>Married Filing Jointly (both under 65)</td><td>$32,200</td><td>$32,200</td></tr><tr><td>Married Filing Jointly (one 65+)</td><td>$33,500</td><td>$33,500</td></tr><tr><td>Head of Household (under 65)</td><td>$24,150</td><td>$24,150</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>If your income is below the standard deduction, you owe $0 federal income tax.</strong></p><p>However, you may still owe FICA (7.65%) regardless of income level, and state tax thresholds may differ.</p><h2 id=\"what-income-pays-37-in-taxes\">What Income Pays 37% in Taxes?</h2><p>The 37% federal tax bracket applies to taxable income over <strong>$626,350</strong> (single filer) or <strong>$751,600</strong> (married filing jointly) in 2026.</p><p><strong>Important</strong>: Only the income ABOVE $626,350 is taxed at 37%. Income below that is taxed at lower rates. A single filer making $700,000 doesn't pay 37% on all $700,000 — only on the $73,650 above the threshold.</p><h2 id=\"what-is-the-60-trap\">What Is the 60% Trap?</h2><p>The \"60% trap\" (or \"tax torpedo\") refers to the situation where retirees face a <strong>marginal tax rate of 40.7% or higher</strong> (instead of the expected 22%) due to Social Security taxation:</p><ol><li>Retiree withdraws from 401(k) → increases taxable income</li><li>Higher income triggers 85% of Social Security to become taxable</li><li>Each additional dollar of 401(k) withdrawal triggers ~$0.85 of Social Security tax</li><li>Effective marginal rate jumps from 22% to <strong>40.7%</strong></li></ol><p><strong>How to avoid it</strong>: Withdraw from Roth accounts (tax-free) to avoid increasing taxable income, or spread withdrawals across multiple years.</p><h2 id=\"what-are-10-types-of-taxable-income\">What Are 10 Types of Taxable Income?</h2><ol><li>Wages and salaries</li><li>Self-employment income</li><li>Investment income (dividends, interest, capital gains)</li><li>Rental income</li><li>Pension and retirement distributions (401k, IRA)</li><li>Social Security benefits (if income exceeds thresholds)</li><li>Unemployment compensation</li><li>Gambling and lottery winnings</li><li>Alimony received (for post-2018 divorces)</li><li>Prizes and awards</li></ol><h2 id=\"can-i-gift-money-to-my-wife\">Can I Gift Money to My Wife?</h2><p><strong>Yes, completely tax-free.</strong> Transfers between spouses are exempt from gift tax. You can transfer any amount to your spouse without tax consequences, as long as:</p><ul><li>Your spouse is a US citizen (unlimited exemption)</li><li>Your spouse is a non-citizen (annual limit of $190,000 in 2026)</li></ul><p>Gifts to others (children, friends) are subject to the $18,000 annual gift exclusion per person.</p><h2 id=\"how-do-i-know-if-i-have-to-pay-taxes\">How Do I Know If I Have to Pay Taxes?</h2><p>You need to file a federal tax return if ANY of these apply:</p><ol><li>Your gross income exceeds the filing threshold ($16,100 single, $32,200 married)</li><li>You had self-employment income of $400 or more</li><li>You owe special taxes (AMT, additional Medicare, household employment)</li><li>You received advance premium tax credits (ACA marketplace)</li><li>You had wages of $108.28 or more from a church</li></ol><p>Even if you don't owe tax, you should file if you're eligible for refundable credits (EITC, Child Tax Credit).</p><h2 id=\"related-resources\">Related Resources</h2><ul><li><a href=\"/paycheck-calculator\">Paycheck Calculator</a></li><li><a href=\"/federal-tax-brackets\">Federal Tax Brackets</a></li><li><a href=\"/blog/how-much-tax-will-i-owe-2026\">How Much Tax Will I Owe?</a></li><li><a href=\"/blog/free-tax-calculator-no-signup-2026\">Free Tax Calculator — No Signup</a></li><li><a href=\"/glossary\">Tax Glossary</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class=\"mx-auto max-w-3xl mt-8\"><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors\" href=\"/blog\">← Back to Blog</a></div></article><section aria-label=\"Author: Rachel Mitchell\" class=\"rounded-xl border border-emerald-500/20 bg-gradient-to-br from-emerald-500/5 to-card/80 p-5 sm:p-6\"><div class=\"flex items-start gap-4\"><img src=\"https://thetaxcalc.com/author-rachel-mitchell.webp\" alt=\"Rachel Mitchell, CPA\" width=\"48\" height=\"48\" class=\"h-12 w-12 shrink-0 rounded-full object-cover ring-1 ring-emerald-500/30\" loading=\"lazy\"/><div class=\"min-w-0 flex-1\"><div class=\"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2\"><a class=\"text-base font-semibold text-foreground hover:text-emerald-400 transition-colors\" href=\"/about#rachel-mitchell\">Rachel Mitchell</a><span class=\"inline-flex items-center rounded-md bg-emerald-500/15 px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-semibold text-emerald-400 ring-1 ring-emerald-500/25\">CPA</span></div><p class=\"mt-0.5 text-sm text-muted-foreground\">Lead Tax Analyst &amp; Editorial Director<!-- -->, TheTaxCalc</p><p class=\"mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground line-clamp-2\">Rachel Mitchell is a Certified Public Accountant (CPA) licensed in Illinois with over 12 years of experience in individual and small-business taxation. She specializes in federal and state income tax compliance, FICA optimization, payroll tax strategy, and multi-state tax planning. Rachel holds an MS in Taxation from Golden Gate University and a BS in Accounting from the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign. She is an active member of the American Institute of Certified Public Accountants (AICPA) and the Illinois CPA Society. Before joining TheTaxCalc, Rachel spent 8 years at a Big Four accounting firm advising high-net-worth clients on tax-efficient wealth strategies.</p><div class=\"mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-1 text-xs text-muted-foreground\"><span class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1\"><svg class=\"h-3 w-3 text-emerald-500/60\" fill=\"none\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" d=\"M6.75 3v2.25M17.25 3v2.25M3 18.75V7.5a2.25 2.25 0 012.25-2.25h13.5A2.25 2.25 0 0121 7.5v11.25m-18 0A2.25 2.25 0 005.25 21h13.5A2.25 2.25 0 0021 18.75m-18 0v-7.5A2.25 2.25 0 015.25 9h13.5A2.25 2.25 0 0121 11.25v7.5\"></path></svg>Reviewed: January 2026</span><span>Tax data verified against IRS Publication 15-T &amp; state revenue departments</span></div></div></div></section><section class=\"mb-8 rounded-xl border border-emerald-500/20 bg-emerald-500/5 p-6\"><h2 class=\"text-lg font-bold text-foreground mb-3\">Try Our Tax Calculators</h2><p class=\"text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-4\">See exactly how much you&#x27;ll take home after all taxes and deductions.</p><div class=\"flex flex-wrap gap-3\"><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-600 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-white hover:bg-emerald-700 transition-colors\" href=\"/paycheck-calculator\">Paycheck Calculator</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-border/50 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/30 transition-colors\" href=\"/illinois-tax-calculator\">IL Calculator</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-border/50 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/30 transition-colors\" href=\"/texas-tax-calculator\">TX Calculator</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-border/50 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/30 transition-colors\" href=\"/florida-tax-calculator\">FL Calculator</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-border/50 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/30 transition-colors\" href=\"/self-employment-tax-calculator\">Self-Employment</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-border/50 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/30 transition-colors\" href=\"/relocation-calculator\">Compare States</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-border/50 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/30 transition-colors\" href=\"/salary\">Salary Calculator</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-border/50 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/30 transition-colors\" href=\"/compare\">Compare Salaries</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-border/50 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/30 transition-colors\" href=\"/glossary\">Tax Glossary</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-border/50 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/30 transition-colors\" href=\"/federal-tax-brackets\">Federal Tax Brackets</a></div></section><section class=\"mb-8 rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-muted/30 p-6\"><h2 class=\"text-lg font-bold text-foreground mb-3\">Popular Salary Calculations</h2><p class=\"text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-4\">Quick access to take-home pay estimates for common salary levels.</p><div class=\"flex flex-wrap gap-3\"><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-emerald-500/20 bg-emerald-500/5 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/40 hover:bg-emerald-500/10 transition-colors\" href=\"/salary/50000\">$50,000</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-emerald-500/20 bg-emerald-500/5 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/40 hover:bg-emerald-500/10 transition-colors\" href=\"/salary/75000\">$75,000</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-emerald-500/20 bg-emerald-500/5 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/40 hover:bg-emerald-500/10 transition-colors\" href=\"/salary/100000\">$100,000</a><a class=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-emerald-500/20 bg-emerald-500/5 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-foreground hover:border-emerald-500/40 hover:bg-emerald-500/10 transition-colors\" href=\"/salary/150000\">$150,