lator 2026: How Much You Actually Keep\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-xs text-muted-foreground line-clamp-2\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Complete take-home pay guide for 2026. Calculate your net pay after federal, FICA, and state taxes. Real examples for every state. Free calculator, no signup.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mt-2 flex flex-wrap gap-1\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"take\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"take\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"home\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"home\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"pay\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"pay\\\"}]]}]]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L11\\\",\\\"sales-tax-by-state-guide-2026\\\",{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/blog/sales-tax-by-state-guide-2026\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"group rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-card p-5 hover:border-emerald-500/30 hover:shadow-md transition-all\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$L2b\\\",\\\"$L2c\\\",\\\"$L2d\\\",\\\"$L2e\\\"]}]]}]]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"2f:T29c1,\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"\\u003cp\\u003e\\\"What do you get taxed on?\\\" is one of the most common tax questions in America. The answer is more complex than you might think — some income is fully taxable, some is partially taxable, and some is completely tax-free. This 2026 guide explains every type of taxable and tax-exempt income, with clear examples.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"official-source\\\"\\u003eOfficial Source\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eTaxable income rules are defined by the IRS in \\u003ca href=\\\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525\\\" rel=\\\"noopener noreferrer\\\"\\u003ePublication 525\\u003c/a\\u003e and \\u003ca href=\\\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p17\\\" rel=\\\"noopener noreferrer\\\"\\u003ePublication 17\\u003c/a\\u003e. Tax-exempt income is listed in \\u003ca href=\\\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525#en_US_2024_publink1000229723\\\" rel=\\\"noopener noreferrer\\\"\\u003eIRS Publication 525, Chapter 2\\u003c/a\\u003e.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"what-items-do-you-pay-taxes-on\\\"\\u003eWhat Items Do You Pay Taxes On?\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"fully-taxable-income\\\"\\u003eFully Taxable Income\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThese income types are 100% subject to federal income tax:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003col\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eWages and salaries\\u003c/strong\\u003e — All employment income (W-2)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSelf-employment income\\u003c/strong\\u003e — 1099 income, freelance, contractor (minus business expenses)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eBonuses and commissions\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Taxed as ordinary income\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eTips\\u003c/strong\\u003e — All tips over $20/month per employer\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eVacation pay\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Taxed as wages\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSeverance pay\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Taxed as wages\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eUnemployment compensation\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Fully taxable at federal level (some states exempt)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eCapital gains\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Profit from selling investments (short-term at ordinary rates, long-term at 0/15/20%)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eDividends\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Qualified (15/20%) and ordinary (ordinary income rate)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eInterest income\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Bank interest, bond interest (some municipal bonds exempt)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eRental income\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Rent received (minus expenses)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003ePension income\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Most pensions are fully taxable\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003e401(k)/Traditional IRA withdrawals\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Fully taxable as ordinary income\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eAnnuities\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Portion of annuity payments (depends on basis)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eGambling winnings\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Fully taxable (lottery, casino, sports betting)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eAlimony received\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Taxable for divorces finalized after 2018\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eJury duty pay\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Taxable as miscellaneous income\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003ePrizes and awards\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Fair market value is taxable\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eCancellation of debt\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Generally taxable (exceptions exist)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eRoyalties\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Taxable as ordinary income\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ol\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"partially-taxable-income\\\"\\u003ePartially Taxable Income\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003col\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSocial Security benefits\\u003c/strong\\u003e — 0%, 50%, or 85% taxable depending on combined income\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eAnnuities\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Only the earnings portion is taxable (return of principal is tax-free)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eNon-qualified dividends\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Taxed at ordinary rate (qualified at lower rate)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eScholarships\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Tuition/fees tax-free; room/board taxable\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ol\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"what-income-is-exempt-from-taxes\\\"\\u003eWhat Income Is Exempt From Taxes?\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"tax-free-income-federal\\\"\\u003eTax-Free Income (Federal)\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThese income types are \\u003cstrong\\u003enot subject to federal income tax\\u003c/strong\\u003e:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003col\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eRoth IRA withdrawals\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Qualified withdrawals (59.5+ and 5-year rule) are 100% tax-free\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eRoth 401(k) withdrawals\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Same as Roth IRA\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eMunicipal bond interest\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Interest from state/local government bonds is federal tax-free\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSocial Security\\u003c/strong\\u003e (low income) — If combined income \\u003c $25,000 (single) or $32,000 (married)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eVeterans' benefits\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Disability compensation, pensions, and education benefits\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eWorkers' compensation\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Job-related injury/illness benefits\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eChild support payments\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Not taxable to the recipient\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eGifts received\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Gifts under $18,000/year per giver (2026)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eInheritances\\u003c/strong\\u003e — No federal income tax on inherited money\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eLife insurance proceeds\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Not taxable as income to beneficiary\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eScholarships\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Tuition, fees, books, supplies (not room/board)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eWelfare benefits\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Public assistance payments\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSupplemental Security Income (SSI)\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Not taxable\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eFoster care payments\\u003c/strong\\u003e — For qualified foster individuals\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eDisability insurance\\u003c/strong\\u003e — If premiums paid with after-tax dollars\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eIRS tax refunds\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Federal tax refunds are not taxable income\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eCombat pay\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Military pay earned in a combat zone\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eForeign income exclusion\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Up to $130,000 (2026) if living abroad\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eHome sale exclusion\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Up to $250,000 (single) / $500,000 (married) profit tax-free\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eHSA withdrawals\\u003c/strong\\u003e — For qualified medical expenses\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ol\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"what-are-the-12-types-of-taxes-in-the-us\\\"\\u003eWhat Are the 12 Types of Taxes in the US?\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cdiv class=\\\"table-wrapper\\\"\\u003e\\u003ctable\\u003e\\u003cthead\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003e#\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eTax Type\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eWho Pays\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eRate\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/thead\\u003e\\u003ctbody\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e1\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eFederal income tax\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eAll earners\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e10-37%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e2\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eState income tax\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eResidents of 41 states\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e0-13.3%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e3\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eFICA (Social Security)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eAll employees\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e6.2% (employer matches)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e4\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eMedicare tax\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eAll employees\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e1.45% (employer matches)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e5\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eState sales tax\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eConsumers in 45 states\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e0-9.56%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e6\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eProperty tax\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eProperty owners\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e0.28-2.49%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e7\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eCapital gains tax\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eInvestors\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e0/15/20%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e8\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eEstate tax\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eEstates over $13.99M\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e40%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e9\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eGift tax\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eGifts over $18,000/year\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e18-40%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e10\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eExcise tax\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eConsumers (gas, alcohol, tobacco)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eVaries\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e11\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eSelf-employment tax\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eSelf-employed\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e15.3%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e12\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eCorporate income tax\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eC-Corporations\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e21% (federal)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/tbody\\u003e\\u003c/table\\u003e\\u003c/div\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"what-is-the-minimum-salary-to-pay-income-tax\\\"\\u003eWhat Is the Minimum Salary to Pay Income Tax?\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eYou must file a tax return (and potentially pay tax) if your income exceeds:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cdiv class=\\\"table-wrapper\\\"\\u003e\\u003ctable\\u003e\\u003cthead\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eFiling Status\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003e2026 Filing Threshold\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eStandard Deduction\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/thead\\u003e\\u003ctbody\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eSingle (under 65)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$16,100\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$16,100\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eSingle (65+)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$17,700\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$17,700\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eMarried Filing Jointly (both under 65)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$32,200\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$32,200\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eMarried Filing Jointly (one 65+)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$33,500\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$33,500\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eHead of Household (under 65)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$24,150\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$24,150\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/tbody\\u003e\\u003c/table\\u003e\\u003c/div\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eIf your income is below the standard deduction, you owe $0 federal income tax.\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eHowever, you may still owe FICA (7.65%) regardless of income level, and state tax thresholds may differ.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"what-income-pays-37-in-taxes\\\"\\u003eWhat Income Pays 37% in Taxes?\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe 37% federal tax bracket applies to taxable income over \\u003cstrong\\u003e$626,350\\u003c/strong\\u003e (single filer) or \\u003cstrong\\u003e$751,600\\u003c/strong\\u003e (married filing jointly) in 2026.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eImportant\\u003c/strong\\u003e: Only the income ABOVE $626,350 is taxed at 37%. Income below that is taxed at lower rates. A single filer making $700,000 doesn't pay 37% on all $700,000 — only on the $73,650 above the threshold.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"what-is-the-60-trap\\\"\\u003eWhat Is the 60% Trap?\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe \\\"60% trap\\\" (or \\\"tax torpedo\\\") refers to the situation where retirees face a \\u003cstrong\\u003emarginal tax rate of 40.7% or higher\\u003c/strong\\u003e (instead of the expected 22%) due to Social Security taxation:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003col\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eRetiree withdraws from 401(k) → increases taxable income\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eHigher income triggers 85% of Social Security to become taxable\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eEach additional dollar of 401(k) withdrawal triggers ~$0.85 of Social Security tax\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eEffective marginal rate jumps from 22% to \\u003cstrong\\u003e40.7%\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ol\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow to avoid it\\u003c/strong\\u003e: Withdraw from Roth accounts (tax-free) to avoid increasing taxable income, or spread withdrawals across multiple years.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"what-are-10-types-of-taxable-income\\\"\\u003eWhat Are 10 Types of Taxable Income?\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003col\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eWages and salaries\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eSelf-employment income\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eInvestment income (dividends, interest, capital gains)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eRental income\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003ePension and retirement distributions (401k, IRA)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eSocial Security benefits (if income exceeds thresholds)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eUnemployment compensation\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eGambling and lottery winnings\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eAlimony received (for post-2018 divorces)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003ePrizes and awards\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ol\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"can-i-gift-money-to-my-wife\\\"\\u003eCan I Gift Money to My Wife?\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eYes, completely tax-free.\\u003c/strong\\u003e Transfers between spouses are exempt from gift tax. You can transfer any amount to your spouse without tax consequences, as long as:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eYour spouse is a US citizen (unlimited exemption)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eYour spouse is a non-citizen (annual limit of $190,000 in 2026)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eGifts to others (children, friends) are subject to the $18,000 annual gift exclusion per person.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"how-do-i-know-if-i-have-to-pay-taxes\\\"\\u003eHow Do I Know If I Have to Pay Taxes?\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eYou need to file a federal tax return if ANY of these apply:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003col\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eYour gross income exceeds the filing threshold ($16,100 single, $32,200 married)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eYou had self-employment income of $400 or more\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eYou owe special taxes (AMT, additional Medicare, household employment)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eYou received advance premium tax credits (ACA marketplace)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eYou had wages of $108.28 or more from a church\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ol\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eEven if you don't owe tax, you should file if you're eligible for refundable credits (EITC, Child Tax Credit).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"related-resources\\\"\\u003eRelated Resources\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/paycheck-calculator\\\"\\u003ePaycheck Calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/federal-tax-brackets\\\"\\u003eFederal Tax Brackets\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/blog/how-much-tax-will-i-owe-2026\\\"\\u003eHow Much Tax Will I Owe?\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/blog/free-tax-calculator-no-signup-2026\\\"\\u003eFree Tax Calculator — No Signup\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/glossary\\\"\\u003eTax Glossary\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"29:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mx-auto max-w-3xl lg:mx-0 lg:flex-1\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"prose-container\\\",\\\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\\\":{\\\"__html\\\":\\\"$2f\\\"}}]}]\\n2a:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mx-auto max-w-3xl mt-8\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L11\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/blog\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"← Back to Blog\\\"}]}]\\n2b:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"flex items-center gap-2 mb-2\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border px-2 py-0.5 text-[10px] font-medium bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800 dark:bg-emerald-500/20 dark:text-emerald-400 border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-500/30\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Tax Guide\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"June 1, 2026\\\"}]]}]\\n2c:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"h3\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-sm font-semibold text-foreground group-hover:text-emerald-600 dark:group-hover:text-emerald-400 transition-colors line-clamp-2 mb-1.5\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Sales Tax by State: Complete Guide to US Tax Rates in 2026\\\"}]\\n2d:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-xs text-muted-foreground line-clamp-2\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Complete guide to sales tax rates for all 50 US states in 2026. State rates, local rates, combined rates, tax-exempt items, and how to calculate sales tax. Free calculator included.\\\"}]\\n2e:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mt-2 flex flex-wrap gap-1\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"sales tax\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"sales tax\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"state sales tax\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"state sales tax\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"sales tax rates\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"sales tax rates\\\"}]]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1c:[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"meta\\\",\\\"0\\\",{\\\"charSet\\\":\\\"utf-8\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"meta\\\",\\\"1\\\",{\\\"name\\\":\\\"viewport\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\\\"}]]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"30:I[27201,[\\\"/_next/static/chunks/ff1a16fafef87110.js\\\",\\\"/_next/static/chunks/61cc43b3d7ad2990.js\\\"],\\\"IconMark\\\"]\\n1a:null\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1e:[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"title\\\",\\\"0\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"What Is Taxable Income? Complete Guide (2026) | TheTaxCalc\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"meta\\\",\\\"1\\\",{\\\"name\\\":\\\"description\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"What do you get taxed on? 20 types of taxable income, 20 types of tax-free income, 12 types of taxes, minimum salary to pay tax, and the 60% trap explained.